@meta
id: 21-15
title: Write the macro instead of performing it
chapter: 21
type: lesson
skills: macro-edit, macro-count
par: 76
minutes: 7
vscode: nothing; a keyboard-macro you can type as text does not exist
open: shipping-zones.ts

@brief
Twelve shipping zones, pasted as `zone,rate`, each to become a record:

    uk,4.99    →    { zone: 'uk', rate: 4.99 },

You know exactly what the macro is before you start; you have recorded
this shape four times already this chapter. So do not record it.
Write it. A register can be assigned on the command line, and the
notation is the one `:reg` shows you, with one addition for the keys
that have no printable form.

@keys
| `:let @a="..."` | put a string into register `a` |
| `\<Esc>` | inside double quotes, the `Esc` key |
| `:reg a` | check what went in |
| `12@a` | run it |

@hints
The macro on one line is `^i{ zone: '` `Esc` `f,s', rate: ` `Esc`
`A },` `Esc` `j`. Write that out with `\<Esc>` wherever you would press
`Esc`, in double quotes, and hand it to `:let @a=`.
--
`:let @a="^i{ zone: '\<Esc>f,s', rate: \<Esc>A },\<Esc>j"` and Enter.
Then `j` and `12@a`.
--
If it does something odd, `:reg a` shows what actually went in. The
usual mistake is a `\<Esc>` inside single quotes, where it is seven
plain characters.

@solution
    :let @a="^i{ zone: '\<Esc>f,s', rate: \<Esc>A },\<Esc>j"<Enter>   j   12@a   :wq<Enter>

Sixty-six keystrokes, about the same as recording it. That is not the
point.

Recording is the right way to build a macro when you are working it
out as you go. Writing it is the right way when it already exists as
text: in a ticket, in a message from a colleague, in your own notes
from last month. `:let @a=` takes it in from the command line, and the
same line can go in your vimrc so the macro is there every morning,
which chapter 28 will come back to.

The `\<Esc>` notation is worth learning properly because it is used
all over vim. Inside a double-quoted string, `\<Esc>`, `\<CR>`,
`\<C-v>` and so on are the named keys. Inside single quotes nothing is
special, which is safer for patterns with backslashes in them and
useless for keys. When the macro has an `Esc` in it, use double quotes.

The last lesson pasted a macro into the file to fix a letter. This one
skips the file. For a short macro with a small mistake, retyping the
whole `:let` is often quicker than paste, fix and yank. For a long
one, the buffer wins. Both end in the same register.

@start shipping-zones.ts
export const shippingZones = [
  uk,4.99
  ie,6.50
  fr,8.99
  de,8.99
  nl,8.99
  be,8.99
  es,10.99
  it,10.99
  pt,10.99
  us,14.99
  ca,14.99
  au,22.99
];

@expected shipping-zones.ts
export const shippingZones = [
  { zone: 'uk', rate: 4.99 },
  { zone: 'ie', rate: 6.50 },
  { zone: 'fr', rate: 8.99 },
  { zone: 'de', rate: 8.99 },
  { zone: 'nl', rate: 8.99 },
  { zone: 'be', rate: 8.99 },
  { zone: 'es', rate: 10.99 },
  { zone: 'it', rate: 10.99 },
  { zone: 'pt', rate: 10.99 },
  { zone: 'us', rate: 14.99 },
  { zone: 'ca', rate: 14.99 },
  { zone: 'au', rate: 22.99 },
];
